--TEST--
`Core\Server::traceId` throws a `LogicError` inside a spawned child while its parent is answering a request, and the parent still reads its own id after the child ends — `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`
--GET--
page=2
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `spawn script` needs `script.spawn`, and a case with no configuration cannot
# spawn at all. This grants that and nothing else.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true
--FILE child.nvs--
<?nvs
// `traceId` is the one member of `Core\Server` that reads the request. A child
// answers no request, so the call throws, and the message names the member.
string $said = "";
try {
    echo Core\Server::traceId(), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $none) {
    $said = $none->message;
}
echo "the child's traceId threw: ",
    Core\Str::contains($said, "Core\\Server::traceId(): there is no request here") ? "yes" : "no",
    "\n";
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The parent answers the `--GET--` request above, so it has an id. Without a
// request the parent would throw too, and the case could not tell the two apart.
string $before = Core\Server::traceId();
echo "the parent has an id of ", Core\Str::length($before), " characters\n";

var $child = await spawn script "child.nvs" with(output: "capture");
echo "ok=", $child->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";
echo $child->output;

// The child's refused call changed nothing in the parent's request.
echo "the parent's id is the same afterwards: ",
    Core\Server::traceId() == $before ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
--EXPECT--
the parent has an id of 32 characters
ok=true
the child's traceId threw: yes
the parent's id is the same afterwards: yes
